# Creating Animated Route Markers with MapboxGL  

One of the most compelling ways to visualize movement data is to show it directly on a map as animated markers traveling along a path. Whether you’re tracking delivery vans, public transit vehicles, or wildlife migration, MapboxGL provides the tools to create smooth, real-time animations that enhance spatial storytelling. This tutorial walks through setting up an animated marker that moves along a predefined route, reacts to live location updates, and adjusts its orientation to match the direction of travel.  

## Preparing the Map and Route Data  

Begin by initializing your MapboxGL map with the desired style and center point. Your route data should be in GeoJSON format, containing a `LineString` geometry that defines the path. This route will serve as the reference for placing and moving the marker.  

```javascript
mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN';
const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v11',
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 13
});

const route = {
  "type": "Feature",
  "geometry": {
    "type": "LineString",
    "coordinates": [
      [-122.420679, 37.772537],
      [-122.425, 37.7749],
      [-122.426, 37.778],
      // more coordinates...
    ]
  }
};
```

Once the map loads, add the route as a layer so you can visualize it as a polyline.  

```javascript
map.on('load', () => {
  map.addSource('route', {
    'type': 'geojson',
    'data': route
  });
  map.addLayer({
    'id': 'route',
    'type': 'line',
    'source': 'route',
    'paint': {
      'line-color': '#007cbf',
      'line-width': 4
    }
  });
});
```

## Creating the Marker Element  

For an animated appearance, you can use a custom HTML element or an image as the marker. A common approach is to create a DOM element with a background image of a vehicle or arrow.  

```javascript
const markerEl = document.createElement('div');
markerEl.className = 'vehicle-marker';

const marker = new mapboxgl.Marker(markerEl)
  .setLngLat(route.geometry.coordinates[0])
  .addTo(map);
```

You can style `.vehicle-marker` in CSS to control its size, shape, and image.  

## Animating Along the Path  

To animate along the route, interpolate between coordinates over time. One approach is to use `requestAnimationFrame` to update the marker’s position on each frame.  

```javascript
let progress = 0; // from 0 to 1
function animateMarker(timestamp) {
  progress += 0.0005; // adjust speed
  if (progress > 1) progress = 0;

  const coordIndex = Math.floor(progress * (route.geometry.coordinates.length - 1));
  const nextIndex = coordIndex + 1;
  const start = route.geometry.coordinates[coordIndex];
  const end = route.geometry.coordinates[nextIndex] || start;

  const lng = start[0] + (end[0] - start[0]) * (progress * (route.geometry.coordinates.length - 1) - coordIndex);
  const lat = start[1] + (end[1] - start[1]) * (progress * (route.geometry.coordinates.length - 1) - coordIndex);

  marker.setLngLat([lng, lat]);
  adjustMarkerRotation(start, end);

  requestAnimationFrame(animateMarker);
}

function adjustMarkerRotation(start, end) {
  const dx = end[0] - start[0];
  const dy = end[1] - start[1];
  const rotation = Math.atan2(dy, dx) * (180 / Math.PI);
  marker.getElement().style.transform = `rotate(${rotation}deg)`;
}

requestAnimationFrame(animateMarker);
```

Here, `adjustMarkerRotation` ensures the marker points in the direction of travel, giving a realistic motion effect.  

## Handling Real-Time Location Updates  

If you have live GPS data, you can replace the static route with incoming coordinates. For example, when receiving a new location via WebSocket or an API, update the marker’s position and optionally append the point to a growing `LineString` to display the traveled path.  

```javascript
function updateVehicleLocation(newCoord) {
  marker.setLngLat(newCoord);

  const routeData = map.getSource('route')._data;
  routeData.geometry.coordinates.push(newCoord);
