# Designing Accessible Dropdown Menus with HTML and Vanilla JavaScript

Creating accessible dropdown menus is a vital part of building inclusive web applications. A well-designed dropdown ensures that all users—including those relying on assistive technologies—can navigate and interact with your interface effectively. This post outlines best practices for implementing dropdown menus in plain HTML and vanilla JavaScript, with emphasis on keyboard navigation, ARIA attributes, and semantic markup, aligning with WCAG (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines) standards.

## 1. Start with Semantic Markup

The foundation of accessibility lies in using correct HTML elements. For a dropdown menu, the `<select>` element with `<option>` children is inherently accessible and recognized by assistive technologies. However, when building custom dropdowns, you must replicate this semantic clarity:

```html
<div class="dropdown" role="listbox" aria-label="Options">
  <button aria-haspopup="listbox" aria-expanded="false" id="dropdownButton">
    Choose an option
  </button>
  <ul id="dropdownList" role="listbox" tabindex="-1" hidden>
    <li role="option" aria-selected="false">Option 1</li>
    <li role="option" aria-selected="false">Option 2</li>
    <li role="option" aria-selected="false">Option 3</li>
  </ul>
</div>
```

Here, `role="listbox"` and `role="option"` convey the purpose of elements to screen readers. The `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` attributes describe the list to users who cannot see it.

## 2. Implement Keyboard Navigation

Keyboard accessibility is non-negotiable. Users should be able to open, navigate, and select items without a mouse. Typical behaviors include:

- **Tab**: Moves focus to the dropdown button.
- **Enter** or **Space**: Opens the dropdown list.
- **Up/Down Arrow Keys**: Navigates between options.
- **Enter**: Selects the highlighted option.
- **Escape**: Closes the dropdown.

Example JavaScript for navigation:

```javascript
const button = document.getElementById('dropdownButton');
const list = document.getElementById('dropdownList');
let options = list.querySelectorAll('[role="option"]');
let currentIndex = -1;

button.addEventListener('click', () => {
  const expanded = button.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';
  button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', !expanded);
  list.hidden = expanded;
  if (!expanded) list.focus();
});

list.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  if (e.key === 'ArrowDown') {
    currentIndex = (currentIndex + 1) % options.length;
    highlightOption();
  } else if (e.key === 'ArrowUp') {
    currentIndex = (currentIndex - 1 + options.length) % options.length;
    highlightOption();
  } else if (e.key === 'Enter') {
    selectOption();
  } else if (e.key === 'Escape') {
    closeDropdown();
  }
});

function highlightOption() {
  options.forEach((opt, i) => {
    opt.classList.toggle('highlight', i === currentIndex);
    opt.setAttribute('aria-selected', i === currentIndex);
  });
}

function selectOption() {
  const selected = options[currentIndex];
  button.textContent = selected.textContent;
  closeDropdown();
}

function closeDropdown() {
  button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');
  list.hidden = true;
  button.focus();
}
```

This script ensures that keyboard users can navigate options, receive visual and programmatic feedback via `aria-selected`, and select items seamlessly.

## 3. Use Appropriate ARIA Attributes

ARIA attributes bridge the gap when native elements are replaced with custom markup. Key attributes include:

- **aria-haspopup**: Indicates that the button triggers a listbox.
- **aria-expanded**: Communicates whether the dropdown is open.
- **aria-selected**: Marks the currently chosen option.
- **aria-activedescendant**: Can be used to associate the listbox with the currently focused option.

Ensure that these attributes update dynamically as the user interacts with the dropdown.

## 4. Provide Visual Focus Indicators

WCAG requires visible focus indicators for interactive elements. Use CSS to highlight the active option and focused button:

```css
[role="option"].highlight {
  background-color: #e0e0e0;
}
button:focus {
  outline: 2px solid #005fcc;
}
```

This benefits both sighted keyboard users and those with cognitive impairments who rely on visual cues.

## 5. Test with Assistive Technologies

After implementation, test your dropdown with screen readers (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver) and keyboard-only navigation. Validate against WCAG guidelines, ensuring that:

- All functionality is available via keyboard.
- The dropdown state is communicated programmatically.
- Focus management is logical and predictable.

---

By combining semantic HTML, robust keyboard support, and well-implemented ARIA attributes, you can create dropdown menus that are both functional and accessible. This approach ensures compliance with WCAG standards and delivers a better experience for all users, regardless of ability.

