# Batch Renaming Image Files with `std::filesystem` in Modern C++

Managing large collections of images often requires consistent and descriptive file naming. Whether you’re organizing photos from a trip or preparing assets for a project, renaming files in bulk can save significant time. Modern C++ offers powerful tools for filesystem interaction through the `<filesystem>` library, enabling developers to iterate through directories and manipulate file names with clear, type-safe code.

In this post, we’ll explore how to use `std::filesystem` to traverse a directory of image files and apply simple batch renaming operations based on a pattern.

---

## Setting Up the Environment

To follow along, you’ll need a C++17 (or newer) compiler, as `std::filesystem` was standardized in C++17. Begin by including the necessary headers:

```cpp
#include <filesystem>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
```

We’ll use the `std::filesystem` namespace for convenience:

```cpp
namespace fs = std::filesystem;
```

---

## Iterating Through Directory Entries

The core of our task is to iterate through all files in a given directory. With `std::filesystem`, this is straightforward:

```cpp
fs::path image_dir = "/path/to/images";

for (const auto& entry : fs::directory_iterator(image_dir)) {
    if (entry.is_regular_file()) {
        // Process each file
    }
}
```

Here, `fs::directory_iterator` provides a range-based interface over directory contents, allowing us to use a `for` loop directly. The check `entry.is_regular_file()` ensures we skip subdirectories or special files.

---

## Filtering for Image Files

Most directories contain a mix of files. We want to focus on images—say, JPEG and PNG formats. We can filter by file extension:

```cpp
auto ext = entry.path().extension();
if (ext == ".jpg" || ext == ".jpeg" || ext == ".png") {
    // This is an image file we want to rename
}
```

Using `fs::path` methods like `.extension()` and `.stem()` (for the base name without extension) makes working with file names intuitive.

---

## Applying a Renaming Pattern

Suppose we want to rename files to include a prefix and a sequential number, maintaining their original extension. First, we’ll set up a counter:

```cpp
int counter = 1;
```

Inside our loop, after confirming the file is an image, we construct the new name:

```cpp
fs::path old_path = entry.path();
std::string new_name = "holiday_" + std::to_string(counter) + old_path.extension().string();
fs::path new_path = old_path.parent_path() / new_name;
```

The `parent_path()` method retrieves the directory containing the file, so we can build the new path in the same location.

---

## Performing the Rename

Renaming is as simple as:

```cpp
fs::rename(old_path, new_path);
++counter;
```

`fs::rename` will move the file to the new path. If you prefer to copy and leave the original intact, `fs::copy_file` is available.

---

## Handling Errors

Filesystem operations can fail—due to permissions, existing files, or invalid paths. Wrapping the loop in a `try` block and catching `fs::filesystem_error` ensures graceful handling:

```cpp
try {
    for (const auto& entry : fs::directory_iterator(image_dir)) {
        if (entry.is_regular_file()) {
            auto ext = entry.path().extension();
            if (ext == ".jpg" || ext == ".jpeg" || ext == ".png") {
                fs::path old_path = entry.path();
                std::string new_name = "holiday_" + std::to_string(counter) + old_path.extension().string();
                fs::path new_path = old_path.parent_path() / new_name;
                fs::rename(old_path, new_path);
                ++counter;
            }
        }
    }
} catch (const fs::filesystem_error& e) {
    std::cerr << "Filesystem error: " << e.what() << '\n';
}
```

---

## Conclusion

With `std::filesystem`, batch renaming image files becomes a concise and readable process. By combining directory iteration, filtering by extension, and constructing new names with string operations, you can quickly reorganize large sets of images according to your needs. This approach is both portable and efficient, leveraging modern C++’s standard library capabilities without relying on platform-specific APIs or external tools.

Whether you’re tidying up a personal gallery or preparing assets for deployment, mastering these filesystem techniques will streamline your workflow and give you greater control over your file organization.

